ed  instances wide so here there are some  main components of Brooklyn zookeeper as  I mentioned before is used to store all  of the metadata as well as do some  coordination the data stream management  service is a rest endpoint that allows  users to dynamically act upon certain  data pipelines like create update read  delete pause partitions pause data  pipelines resume them and also to  retrieve like statistics about the  pipeline's we can also do that  dynamically each Brooklyn instance has  what is known as the coordinator which  is the brains of the system which is  responsible for the leader is  responsible for calculating and  distributing the tasks the coordinators  the other coordinators are responsible  for reading these data stream tasks and  then sort of distributing them to where  necessary  each Brooklyn instance can be configured  with any number of consumers as in if  you want to read from espresso Oracle  Kafka event hubs you would configure  your Brooklyn cluster to have four  different types of consumers in the same  respect Brooklyn can also be configured  with any number of different types of  producers so if you need to write to any  variety of destinations like event hubs  can  Kafka you can just configure your  Brooklyn cluster with those producers  and these consumer and producer  api's are standardized in a way that  it's very easy to extend these API is to  easily add support for new sources and  destinations which was one of the main  goals that we had when we started  Brooklyn so now that you have a an idea  of the architecture I'll talk about  where Brooklyn is today and what plans  we have going forward for it  currently Brooklyn supports a variety of  consumers we have espresso Oracle Kafka  event hubs and Kinesis and it also  supports two destinations namely Kafka  and event hubs some of the features of  Brooklyn are that first is that it's  multi tenant it can power thousands of  data streams across various sources and  destinations we do guarantee at least  once delivery meaning that we guarantee  that  each event is guaranteed at least once  to the destination and ordering of these  events are guaranteed and maintained at  a per partition level for the huge Kafka  mirroring use case to replace Kafka  mirror maker we had to add finer support  finer control of data pipelines like the  ability to pause and auto pause  partitions and we've also introduced  some features that improve the latency  such as something called fleshless  produce mode wherein we avoid calling  producer dot flesch which is a blocking  call on the Kafka producers and this has  helped improve our latency and what is  known as Kafka rebalances Brooklyn in  production today Brooklyn is in  production today and has been in  production since 2016 and Brooklyn  streams within brooklyn streams with  espresso or oracle as its source we  propagate third over 38 billion messages  a day over 2,000 data streams which is  over 1,000 unique sources because of  that data stream deduplication feature  that I mentioned earlier and we are  supporting over 200 applications within  LinkedIn for Brooklyn  supporting for Brooklyn clusters  mirroring Kafka data we are doing over  three trillion messages a day spanning  over 200 mirroring data pipelines and we  are mirroring tens of thousands of Kafka  topics and I'm happy to announce that  since June of this year  Brooklyn is now open source and is on  github but our work is not done we still  want to add more support for new sources  and destinations namely on the source  side we want to support my sequel cosmos  DB and Azure sequel to name a few and on  the destination side we want to write to  Azure blob storage Kinesis cosmos DB a  sure sequel and Couchbase and the  interesting thing about these  destinations is that some of these are  storage where in previously our  destinations were event hubs and Kafka  so there are more streaming messaging  systems so we'll introduce some new  features in Brooklyn to allow for  batching to support these storage  destinations and we want to optimize  Brooklyn even further we want to add  ability for Brooklyn to auto scale  automatically based on the throughput  needs of the data pipelines in  near-real-time we also want to roll out  this feature called pass-through  compression so which will help the  throughput by at least five X for our  Kafka mirroring scenarios so when you  mirror Kafka data from one cluster to  another it's very simple it's just a we  just want to have a big fat pipe that  mirrors bytes of data from one place to  another but the problem is that the  Kafka consumer which is the you know the  Kafka client in open source Kafka the  coffeehouse consumer what it does is it  automatically DC realises all of the  events that are read and in a tree and  then the Kafka producer Ricci realises  that data when producing to the  destination  so this serialization deserialization  serialization takes up actually over 70%  of our CPU and we don't care about the  message the event boundaries in this  case because we are simply mirroring  we're not doing any data processing  we're not looking into the payload of  these Kafka events so we want to do  what's called pass-through  we're and we disable the deserialization  and the resale is a ssin of these  messages and we simply write bites bites  out and bites in so this would we've  shown that this can give us a 5x  throughput increase another thing that  we want to do is we want to make some  read optimizations where we want to be  able to read once and write to multiple  destinations so in the case that I  mentioned earlier where all of these  application teams are consuming from the  same Kafka destination because they're  all interested in member profile data  there may be some cases where they don't  want to share the same underlying topic  and in this case we want to be able to  read from espresso once and then write  to maybe 10 different Kafka topics but  the challenge with that is that as I  mentioned each application can be at  their own pace so we need to be able to  handle that too lastly I just want to  thank all of you again for choosing to  attend my presentation I think I have  three minutes for any questions but if  you have any questions outside please  feel free to find me or message me on  any of these channels you can message me  on LinkedIn you can send me a direct  email to my LinkedIn email address or  you can ask questions on our github or  sorry on our Gator channel we're really  active on getter and you can check out  our source code on github thank you  [Applause]  yeah so the question is can we can  people easily write their own consumers  and producers too that are maybe outside  of the scope of what we have in the  future and yes the answer is yes we're  open source now so anyone is free to  contribute the api's are pretty standard  so it's I'm sorry what language ok so  right now we support Java yeah Brooklyn  is written completely in Java yep yeah  so the question is that Brooklyn seems  to have a lot of overlap with Kafka  Connect and the museum some of the  features that I think are a little bit  unique about Brooklyn is that so let's  say Kafka Connect for example I think  with Kafka Connect you can configure a  connector with it but if you configure a  source or yeah if you couldn't figure a  source then the sync is automatically  Kafka and if you configure a sync then  the source is automatically Kafka so you  can do either source or destination as  Kafka and then sort the other one can be  something else  whereas Brooklyn can stream anything  from X to Y so we don't even need to  involve Kafka there the museum is from  my understanding it is known more for  change data capture which is you can  easily write plugins to consume from a  variety of databases but Brooklyn can  also read from messaging systems it can  do batching so that it can write back  into data stores and it can do Kafka -  Kafka event hubs - Kinesis I think it's  if I were to summarize I think it's a  little bit more customizable yeah  yeah so yeah what is the question around  yeah so for example so the question is  can I give some examples of policies and  how they are enforced so for example if  you have a particular data set that is  private you can say that this needs to  be encrypted and therefore you can  there's a way for you to in to configure  your data pipelines to let Brooklyn know  that this is an encrypted data stream  and that it may need to read the keys  for that source from a particular key  management service and then also when it  ships the events it needs to also use  that same key to encrypt those messages  and the applications also needs to use  their that key to decrypt it on the  other end and so at LinkedIn we have  services like a key management service  which enables us to do this so it's all  within the configuration of a data  pipeline yep  okay so the question is is there any  initiative to push Brooklyn over to  Apache Apache is something that we did  evaluate but going forward as a company  LinkedIn is is is moving away from  Apache yeah okay thank you please feel  free to reach out to me if you have any  questions thank you  [Applause]
 

